            <ArticleTitle>Failure of surgical decompression for a presumed case of piriformis syndrome. Case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>652-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Diagnosis of piriformis syndrome is difficult and its precise definition is highly controversial. In this article, the authors present the case of a patient who had clinical features suggestive of piriformis syndrome. During surgery the patient was found to have a rare variation in anatomical structures, in which the peroneal nerve was displaced by the piriformis muscle. Surgical decompression did not alleviate the patient's symptoms.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
